    "q-104774": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104774",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/b63554ba2724862e30569687e53e2068eda0024052d49a163b6aa2097c2ba659.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[2,4].$ Notice that $x=3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/357d426d58f6dd77a618fd30ad3735ef6a77763f77387be3181bd5149f973488.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-5-(-1)}{4-2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
