      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/23d22833ae89d7ed3f1c34a07f039031d77223350f0b23d17c6843f67f05668c.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-5g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-5{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=2{f}^{′}(2)-5{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/1de80fcae8f07412e788df9ef202b15f5cdcf9fe8ca9a994abd8b1acd789359f.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{3-1}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =2{f}^{′}(2)-5{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-5\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
