    "q-344888": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344888",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/156dee2459824e3bfefa3b9e252b0f028ccea6d72c6a650808f51daa874bb35f.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)-g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=2{f}^{′}(1)-{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (0,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/690bfc52663b966da350e6fa941057a7b20a2d9547d39cd95782059ee3c44aba.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (0,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-0}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =2{f}^{′}(1)-{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2(-1)-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
