    "q-105500": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105500",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/625de64447d959dab5a8a0da610e8687603454c331bb1c257300f222ab2e7f10.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$ where $h(x)=3f(x)-4g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=3{f}^{′}(x)-4{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-2)=3{f}^{′}(-2)-4{g}^{′}(-2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/d90d88b1e2cff1e08017bde2dd8d615444187f0290704e077f974ec58ee46112.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-2}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{4-2}{0-(-4)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & =3{f}^{′}(-2)-4{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
