      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/5981116f8c9ad6e9dea29da381a0becbd997aa259a232dd10bd7395a78e976ad.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(-1)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(-1)={f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot h(-1)+f(-1)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=1$ and $h(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${h}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(-1) & ={f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot h(-1)+f(-1)\\cdot {h}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
