    "q-344897": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344897",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/7af6b87184781cf0f6a3132f375e7917bf4f4cd8ef8b0a4979ab949ac9d6b6c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $h(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
