    "q-104795": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104795",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/61c9cb5fed816e028cf47e3d6b264a40a2fc4c04bc5d330e8662a8dfd8fa26e1.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=4$ and $g(2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
