    "q-105505": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105505",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/ff4df5dce9f78798b864ea1880990cb91af83c7ec5fd1ec37775d885f72bc250.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=0$ and $g(1)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,5)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,2)$ equals $-3.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-3$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
