    "q-48952": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48952",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/e13e109301fca50036cbf933b2a7946f3f53397f39f5e249c0d64e1f6ae78ba2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})$ where $q(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{3}{2})=1$ and $g(\\frac{3}{2})=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
