    "q-344912": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344912",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/e78aafab1786a1fa7ee8e58c80c41b246179cfcce7399324731131f40e8d1490.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(-1)=-1$ and $g(-1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,1)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-1)\\cdot g(-1)-f(-1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-1)}{(g(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{3}{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
